==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|mate|ship}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|maatschap||partnership}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-mateship.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|countable|and|uncountable|nautical}} The post of [[mate]] on a ship; a posting as mate.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author=William Chambers|author2=Robert Chambers|title=Chambers′ Edinburgh Journal|volume=10|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=EDsxAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA263&dq=%22mateship%22+-australia+-intitle:%22australia|australian%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=Ib2rT_iHEIrumAWIwoTcDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateship%22%20-australia%20-intitle%3A%22australia|australian%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=263
|passage=Paul Jones profited by his brother′s position and counsel to improve himself in navigation and other professional studies, and was so successful in the endeavour, that he was deemed worthy of being appointed, on his return to Whitehaven, to a third '''mateship''' in a vessel in the slave-trade.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|whaling|obsolete}} A type of [[contract]] between ships to cooperate and share the proceeds of an expedition.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1835|author=Francis Hilliard|title=American Law: The Formative Years|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=8-kMYQQOxjwC&pg=PA122&dq=%22mateship%22+-australia+-intitle:%22australia|australian%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=Ib2rT_iHEIrumAWIwoTcDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateship%22%20-australia%20-intitle%3A%22australia|australian%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=122
|passage={{...}}as, for instance, the contract termed '''''mateship''''', by which one whaling-ship meeting another forms a partnership in the proceeds of the expedition.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[fellowship|Fellowship]]; [[companionship]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=G. Firth Scott|title=The Last Lemurian|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=64pUx7Nt-S4C&pg=PA6&dq=%22mateship%22+-australia+-intitle:%22australia|australian%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=Ib2rT_iHEIrumAWIwoTcDw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateship%22%20-australia%20-intitle%3A%22australia|australian%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=6
|passage=“Don′t mind if I do,” I answered, and thus begun a '''mateship''' that in the course of the next few months was to yield to each of us a fairly big experience of adventure, and, what was more acceptable to me, a good round sum in cash.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|Australia|NZ}} [[friendship|Friendship]], particularly between men, such as develops in shared adversity; [[solidarity]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Anna Wierzbicka|title=Understanding Cultures Through Their Key Words: English, Russian, Polish, German, and Japanese|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=6YJjY9qBv9kC&pg=PA117&dq=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=QoqrT4CcDaaPmQWJ69DhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=117
|passage=In traditional Australian culture, “'''mateship'''” was expected to bind people not only with their “best mates” or their “great mates” but also with their fellow-miners, fellow-shearers, fellow-“diggers”, fellow-soldiers, or fellow-footballers, and this expectation is one of this culture′s most enduring and characteristic features.<sup>19</sup>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Graham Seal|title=Inventing Anzac: The Digger And National Mythology|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=Z40eykeP_IMC&pg=PA77&dq=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=QoqrT4CcDaaPmQWJ69DhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=77
|passage=While the camaraderie of war is usually an unspoken assumption, it seems peculiar that '''mateship''', usually considered to be the core of the digger ethos and the ‘spirit of the Anzac’, barely appears in the diggers′ own expressions at this time.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Albert Moran|author2=Errol Vieth|title=The A to Z of Australian and New Zealand Cinema|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=DPyFtajDTmEC&pg=PA186&dq=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=QoqrT4CcDaaPmQWJ69DhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=186
|passage=A significant element of masculinity in Australian cultural history, and therefore Australian film, is '''mateship'''.{{...}}In times of war, '''mateship''' was a measure of the quality of relationship, as a mate was one whom a soldier would happily accompany into the jungle; that is, one who would be dependable and able to offer support.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Rod Giblett|title=People and Places of Nature and Culture|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=gEmdxmjTD84C&pg=PA127&dq=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=QoqrT4CcDaaPmQWJ69DhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateship%22|%22mateships%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=127
|passage=The bush is the crucible of Australian national identity because it is here that '''mateship''', that linchpin of Australian national identity, was forged.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|zoology|psychology|anthropology}} A relationship based on [[mating]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1942|author=Fred August Moss|author2=Edward Lee Thorndike|title=Comparative Psychology|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=JEPWAAAAIAAJ&q=%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&dq=%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=b4yrT9CYFMTSmAXZ48zhBA&redir_esc=y|page=370
|passage=The '''mateships''' of the three last-named animals are solitary, though in the case of wolves, the separated members of various '''mateships''' gather in packs during the winter months.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=David J. Buller|title=Adapting Minds: Evolutionary Psychology And The Persistent Quest For Human Nature|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=dQ5MGDvn8eIC&pg=PA259&dq=%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=b4yrT9CYFMTSmAXZ48zhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateships%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false|page=259
|passage=Further, in most cultures without systems of codified laws, long-term '''mateships''' are ritually sanctioned by the community. If we are not to have too provincial a conception of marriage, these '''mateships''' should also count as marriages.}}
#* '''2010''', A. Irving Hallowell, ''15: The Protocultural Foundations of Human Adaptation'', Yehudi A. Cohen (editor), ''Human Adaptation: The Biosocial Background'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=hW1sPY90eeMC&pg=PA164&dq=%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=b4yrT9CYFMTSmAXZ48zhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateships%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 164],
#*: In Homo sapiens we find two types of polygamous '''mateships''', polygyny and polyandry, and social structures based on these are ordinarily called “families.” Relatively rare in man in an institutionalized form, polyandrous '''mateships''' appear to be absent in infrahuman primates.
#* '''2012''', Dietrich Klusmann, Wolfgang Berner, ''Chapter 14: Sexual Motivation in '''Mateships''' an Sexual Conflict'', Todd K. Shackelford, Aaron T. Goetz (editors), ''The Oxford Handbook of Sexual Conflict in Humans'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=5eQ6ix4r9ikC&pg=PA233&dq=%22mateships%22+-intitle:%22%22+-inauthor:%22%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=b4yrT9CYFMTSmAXZ48zhBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mateships%22%20-intitle%3A%22%22%20-inauthor%3A%22%22&f=false page 233],
#*: The most frequent conflict within human '''mateships''' is the conflict between male sexual persistence and female sexual resistance.

